  "_comment": "PRD P2-10 CAPSTONE \u2014 the cross-SDK dispute PUBLIC-SURFACE manifest. This is the single source of truth for the META parity test: it enumerates EVERY intentional public symbol of the AIP-14b dispute clients and, for each, the TS name + Py name + kind + per-language required arity. BOTH the TS jest META test (sdk-js/tests/dispute-parity.test.ts) and the Py pytest META test (python-sdk-v2/tests/test_cross_sdk/test_dispute_parity.py) load THIS file and assert: (1) every symbol exists in their own SDK with the declared name + required arity, and (2) no symbol is present in only one language (each row carries BOTH a `ts` and a `py` name \u2014 a missing twin is a hard failure). `arity` is the count of REQUIRED logical parameters (excludes `self`/`cls`, excludes optional/default/rest params); a symbol may accept additional OPTIONAL params in either SDK without breaking parity. Framework-inherited members (pydantic model_*, IntEnum/str/dict/Exception builtins) are intentionally EXCLUDED \u2014 only the authored dispute API is listed. Any new public dispute symbol MUST be added here with both twins, or the META test fails. A class may carry an `accessors` array for getter/property members (TS `get x()` / Py `@property`): these are validated via their accessor DESCRIPTOR (TS `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(proto, name).get` is a function; Py `inspect.getattr_static(cls, name)` is a `property`), NOT via prototype value access \u2014 reading a getter off the prototype would trigger it (and throw on an unconfigured facade). Per-SDK construction idiom is intentionally EXCLUDED: TS clients take a single config object + signer; Py takes positional contract/account/w3 and adds `from_config` classmethods, `encode_calldata`, and (EvaluatorClient) a `base_url` property \u2014 these are the standard ethers-vs-web3 idiom, consistently applied, so their absence from a row is NOT a gap. The 4 TS-only evaluate WIRE-PARSE shapes (SignedEvaluateResponse, ProposeDirectlyEvaluateResponse, EvaluateResponse, EvaluatorRuling) are NO LONGER on the public package entrypoint (de-exported from sdk-js/src/index.ts) precisely BECAUSE Py has no twin (it parses the evaluate response as a raw dict); they are listed under `absent` to lock that de-export.",
